Cut native views' holes in the wgpu renderer, so every wgpu target (Linux, Android, web) can show one.

What: `PrimitiveBatch::NativeViews` draws, in paint order, each view's bounds clipped to its content mask as
transparent black with blending off, as the Metal renderer's `native_view_fragment` does (Chromium's
`ReplaceExistingQuadWithHolePunch` semantics): what was painted beneath is erased, what is painted after covers it.
Holes are passed as `Quad`s, so both instance transports (storage buffers, WebGL2's texture) decode them with the
existing `load_quad`; the pipeline tests depth like other non-quad batches and writes none, so opaque quads painted
after a view (drawn early, front to back) stay over its hole. While the last drawn scene shows a native view the
surface uses its transparent alpha mode (premultiplied where offered), whatever the window asked for, and goes back
to the window's own mode once none shows; the mode chosen is logged when views first show (a warning when the
surface offers no premultiplied mode, NVIDIA's Vulkan WSI being the known risk). `set_native_view_hole_inset` cuts
holes inside the views' bounds, for a platform that rounds views to whole logical pixels (Wayland, patch 0049).

New files: `shaders_native_view.wgsl` (concatenated after each transport), `wgpu_renderer/native_view_hole.rs` (the
hole quads, the pipeline's colour target, the alpha-mode log), `wgpu_renderer/tests/native_view_hole.rs` (table test
`native_view_hole_is_cut_in_paint_order`, on the headless renderer). `wgpu_renderer.rs` holds only the hooks.

Why: the wgpu renderer ignored `PrimitiveBatch::NativeViews`, so no wgpu target could show a native view
(xuckless/parallax#1). The renderer is private to gpui_wgpu, so this can't live in a Parallax crate.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

diff --git a/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/shaders_native_view.wgsl b/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/shaders_native_view.wgsl
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/shaders_native_view.wgsl
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+// --- native view holes --- //
+
+// A native view shows through a hole: its bounds, clipped to its content mask, replace
+// whatever was drawn beneath them with transparent black (Chromium's
+// ReplaceExistingQuadWithHolePunch). The pipeline does not blend, so the zero replaces
+// the color, and its depth test keeps opaque quads painted after the view on top. Holes
+// are passed as quads, so both instance transports already decode them.
+
+@vertex
+fn vs_native_view_hole(@builtin(vertex_index) vertex_id: u32, @builtin(instance_index) instance_id: u32) -> @builtin(position) vec4<f32> {
+    let hole = load_quad(instance_id);
+    let unit_vertex = vec2<f32>(f32(vertex_id & 1u), 0.5 * f32(vertex_id & 2u));
+    let clipped = clip_to_mask(unit_vertex, hole.bounds, hole.content_mask);
+    return to_device_position_impl(clipped.position);
+}
+
+@fragment
+fn fs_native_view_hole() -> @location(0) vec4<f32> {
+    return vec4<f32>(0.0);
+}
diff --git a/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/wgpu_renderer/native_view_hole.rs b/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/wgpu_renderer/native_view_hole.rs
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/wgpu_renderer/native_view_hole.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+//! Native views: the hole the renderer cuts where one shows. The platform composites the view
+//! beneath the window (a Wayland subsurface, an Android SurfaceView, a DOM element under the
+//! canvas); every wgpu target shares this hole.
+
+use gpui::{PaintNativeView, Quad, ScaledPixels};
+
+/// The holes of a scene's native views, as quads the hole shader reads with either instance
+/// transport, each cut `inset` inside its view's bounds.
+pub(super) fn holes(native_views: &[PaintNativeView], inset: ScaledPixels) -> Vec<Quad> {
+    native_views
+        .iter()
+        .map(|native_view| Quad {
+            order: native_view.order,
+            bounds: native_view.bounds.inset(inset),
+            content_mask: native_view.content_mask,
+            ..Quad::default()
+        })
+        .collect()
+}
+
+/// No blending, so a hole replaces what was drawn beneath it (Metal's native view pipeline does
+/// the same).
+pub(super) fn color_target(format: wgpu::TextureFormat) -> wgpu::ColorTargetState {
+    wgpu::ColorTargetState {
+        format,
+        blend: None,
+        write_mask: wgpu::ColorWrites::ALL,
+    }
+}
+
+/// Native views need a compositor that blends the window. NVIDIA's Vulkan WSI has been
+/// known to offer only `Opaque` on Wayland, where every hole then shows black.
+pub(super) fn log_alpha_mode(alpha_mode: wgpu::CompositeAlphaMode) {
+    match alpha_mode {
+        wgpu::CompositeAlphaMode::PreMultiplied | wgpu::CompositeAlphaMode::Inherit => {
+            log::info!("native views: window surface alpha mode {alpha_mode:?}")
+        }
+        wgpu::CompositeAlphaMode::Auto
+        | wgpu::CompositeAlphaMode::Opaque
+        | wgpu::CompositeAlphaMode::PostMultiplied => log::warn!(
+            "native views: the window surface offers no premultiplied alpha mode, only \
+             {alpha_mode:?}, so native views show black through their holes"
+        ),
+    }
+}
diff --git a/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/wgpu_renderer/tests/native_view_hole.rs b/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/wgpu_renderer/tests/native_view_hole.rs
